# The graphical desktop: a light blue background and the terminal in a
# window. It is on by default. The screenshots check real pixels: the
# background in the corners (a8d0f0), the window's title bar (3a6ea5) and
# the black inside of the terminal.
$ desktop
desktop: on, the terminal window has 221x56 characters ('desktop off' ends it)
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 0,0=a8d0f0 1919,1079=a8d0f0 30,540=a8d0f0 960,78=3a6ea5 1800,990=000000
$ display
mode: 1920x1080, 32 bits per pixel
text: 221x56 characters (8x16 font)
desktop: on

# The terminal works as before, also when it scrolls.
$ echo héllo from a window
héllo from a window
$ help
$ help
$ help
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 0,0=a8d0f0 1919,1079=a8d0f0 960,78=3a6ea5 1800,120=000000

# Color bars cover everything; afterwards the desktop is back.
$& display test
@screenshot 1920x1080 bars
> x
$wait
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 0,0=a8d0f0 1919,1079=a8d0f0 960,78=3a6ea5

# Another resolution keeps the desktop.
$ display mode 1024x768
text: 114x39 characters (8x16 font)
desktop: on
@screenshot 1024x768 pixels 0,0=a8d0f0 1023,767=a8d0f0 512,56=3a6ea5

# Off: the terminal has the whole screen again, and keeps its text.
$ desktop off
desktop off (RAM disk: not kept after reboot)
$ display
text: 128x48 characters (8x16 font)
desktop: off
@screenshot 1024x768 text
$ desktop
desktop: off ('desktop on' starts it)
$ desktop on
desktop on (RAM disk: not kept after reboot)
@screenshot 1024x768 pixels 0,0=a8d0f0 1023,767=a8d0f0 512,56=3a6ea5
$ desktop sideways
usage: desktop [on | off]
